  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $-2{x}^{3}+16\\ge 0.$\n\n$x\\in $\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{3}+16 & =-2({x}^{3}-8) \\\\  & =-2(x-2)({x}^{2}+2x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2(x-2)({x}^{2}+2x+4).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$ The solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=2.$  This root divides the number line into $2$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,2)\\,,\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+2x+4$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-\\infty ,2).$ \n\nTherefore, remembering that the root is also a solution, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$",
